// Title: High severity incident not picked up within 30 minutes
// Id: SO-003
// Severity: Medium
// Tables: SecurityIncident
// Frequency: PT30M
// Period: PT2H
// Entities: URL(Url=IncidentUrl)
// Custom details: IncidentNumber, IncidentTitle, MinutesOpen, Provider
// Description: A high severity incident is still in status New, has no owner and has not been
//   touched by the Autonomous SOC Analyst (no soc-agent label) half an hour after it was created.
//   It is the safety net for the night shift: if both the agent and the on-call analyst miss an
//   incident, this says so while it still matters.
// False positives: None in the usual sense. If it fires often, the queue is under-staffed or the
//   agent is not running.
// Tuning: The window is 30 to 60 minutes after creation so each incident is reported once; change
//   both numbers and the Frequency together. An incident whose severity is raised to High more than
//   an hour after it was created falls outside the window and is not reported.
// Response: Assign the incident now. Then find out why it was missed: check that the agent's
//   automation rule and playbook ran, and who was on call.
// References: https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/sentinel/incident-investigation
SecurityIncident
| where TimeGenerated > ago(2h)
| summarize arg_max(TimeGenerated, *) by IncidentNumber
| where Severity == "High" and Status == "New"
| where CreatedTime between (ago(60m) .. ago(30m))
| where isempty(tostring(Owner.assignedTo)) and isempty(tostring(Owner.userPrincipalName))
| where tostring(Labels) !has "soc-agent"
// This rule's own incidents are Medium, so it cannot report itself; the title check makes that explicit.
| where Title != "High severity incident not picked up within 30 minutes"
| project CreatedTime, IncidentNumber, IncidentTitle = Title, Severity, Status,
          MinutesOpen = toint((now() - CreatedTime) / 1m), Provider = ProviderName, IncidentUrl
